        "titulo": "Saying what hurts, step by step",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: to say what HURTS, use «wehtun» (separable) with the DATIVE of the person: «Der Kopf tut mir weh» (my head hurts — literally \"the head does me pain\"). STEP 2: plural: «Die Füße tun mir weh» (my feet hurt). STEP 3: ask «Was tut Ihnen weh?» or «Wo tut es weh?» (Where does it hurt?). STEP 4: another way, with «-schmerzen» (aches), just like English \"headache\", \"stomachache\", \"backache\": «Ich habe Kopfschmerzen / Bauchschmerzen / Halsschmerzen / Rückenschmerzen» (a sore throat = Halsschmerzen). STEP 5: «Es tut weh» means \"it hurts\", with no person. STEP 6: remember the dative reflexive from lesson 38: «Ich habe mir die Hand verletzt» (I hurt my hand) — German says \"the hand\", not \"my hand\". Very common in the Perfekt.",
